import{_ as n,o as a,c as s,a0 as t}from"./chunks/framework.C3yZ6M33.js";const u=JSON.parse('{"title":"Event graph model","description":"","frontmatter":{},"headers":[],"relativePath":"explanation/event-graph-model.md","filePath":"explanation/event-graph-model.md"}'),o={name:"explanation/event-graph-model.md"};function i(r,e,p,d,l,c){return a(),s("div",null,[...e[0]||(e[0]=[t('<h1 id="event-graph-model" tabindex="-1">Event graph model <a class="header-anchor" href="#event-graph-model" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Event graph model&quot;">​</a></h1><h2 id="why-events-not-dags" tabindex="-1">Why events, not DAGs <a class="header-anchor" href="#why-events-not-dags" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;Why events, not DAGs&quot;">​</a></h2><p>A DAG (directed acyclic graph) encodes a fixed execution order. You define edges: A → B → C. The runtime walks the graph topologically.</p><p>This works for pipelines but breaks down when:</p><ul><li><strong>Branching depends on results</strong> — you do not know which path to take until a node runs. In a DAG, you would need conditional edges or pre/post hooks.</li><li><strong>The graph changes at runtime</strong> — a node decides it needs 3 more tasks. In a DAG, you would need dynamic subgraphs or a meta-layer.</li><li><strong>Multiple consumers of the same output</strong> — one node result feeds 3 downstream nodes. In a DAG, that is fan-out, but the DAG does not know they are independent.</li></ul><h2 id="the-event-graph" tabindex="-1">The event graph <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-event-graph" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The event graph&quot;">​</a></h2><p>In an event graph, there are no edges. Nodes subscribe to event types (<code>on</code>) and emit event types (<code>emits</code>). The topology emerges from the contracts:</p><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>Node A: on=[start], emits=[a.done]</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span>Node B: on=[a.done], emits=[b.done]</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span>Node C: on=[a.done], emits=[c.done]</span></span></code></pre></div><p>The &quot;edge&quot; A → B is implicit: A emits <code>a.done</code>, B subscribes to <code>a.done</code>. The bus matches them at runtime. No one declares the edge.</p><h2 id="what-this-gives-you" tabindex="-1">What this gives you <a class="header-anchor" href="#what-this-gives-you" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;What this gives you&quot;">​</a></h2><p><strong>Branching is free.</strong> A driver decides what to emit based on its result — <code>params.emit</code> maps return codes onto different event types. The graph needs no conditional edges.</p><p><strong>Fan-out is free.</strong> Every node whose <code>on</code> matches an event is a candidate for the same fire round. The bus is the scheduler.</p><p><strong>Fan-in is free.</strong> <code>mode: all</code> waits for every <code>on</code> pattern to have a match in the log; <code>fan_in:</code> is the count-based join over one pattern. No join nodes or synchronization primitives — see <a href="./../reference/on-grammar.html"><code>on:</code> event patterns</a>.</p><p><strong>Dynamic topology is free.</strong> A driver can emit events that trigger nodes that did not exist when the run started. <code>ctx.inject</code> (and the <code>runner</code> driver built on it) adds nodes to the live graph. See <a href="./self-modifying.html">Self-modifying workflows</a>.</p><p><strong>Wildcards are free.</strong> <code>on: [shard.*.ready]</code> catches any <code>shard.N.ready</code> event — map-reduce without fixed width; the planner decides at runtime how many shards exist. Captures ride into templates as <code>${event.match.N}</code>.</p><h2 id="the-core-loop" tabindex="-1">The core loop <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-core-loop" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The core loop&quot;">​</a></h2><p>The <code>Runner</code> (<code>peregrin-engine</code>) is a sequential, queue-driven graph walker:</p><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>1. Publish `start` (or `seed` a replay log + `check_ready`)</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span>2. Each loop iteration: drain due poll ticks, drain the ingress</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span>   channel into `pending`</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span>3. Run every `ready` (node, triggers) pair in order; collect each</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span>   node&#39;s returned events, `ctx.emit` side events, `ctx.inject` specs</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span>4. Publish pending events → matched nodes become `ready` → loop</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span>5. `workflow.cancel` or a `stop_on` type → `workflow.cancelled`;</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span>   a failure anywhere → `workflow.failed`; queues drain cleanly →</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span>   `workflow.done`</span></span></code></pre></div><p>v1 interleaved <code>ready</code> batches through <code>Effect.forEach</code> with unbounded concurrency, but all cross-node state was only touched at yield points — the sequential batch walk is semantically equivalent and deterministic. Poll nodes don&#39;t sleep the loop: <code>&lt;id&gt;.tick</code> re-fires sit on a deadline heap that drains at the top of each iteration.</p><p>The ingress channel is how external producers (webhook listeners, WAL pollers, the supersede queue&#39;s cancel signal) feed a live run without <code>&amp;mut</code> access to the bus — injected events serialize at the next iteration boundary. See <a href="./../reference/queue-supersede.html">Queue &amp; supersede</a>.</p><h2 id="the-primitives" tabindex="-1">The primitives <a class="header-anchor" href="#the-primitives" aria-label="Permalink to &quot;The primitives&quot;">​</a></h2><div class="language- vp-adaptive-theme"><button title="Copy Code" class="copy"></button><span class="lang"></span><pre class="shiki shiki-themes github-light github-dark vp-code" tabindex="0"><code><span class="line"><span>Event    — the flat envelope: type, payload, source, id, ts.</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span>           The unit of flow and state (peregrin-core).</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span>Campaign — name, config, capabilities, the node list (peregrin-core).</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span>Node     — subscribes (on) + produces (emits) + runs via a driver.</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span>Driver   — executes the action (shell, agent, git, …) — one unified</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span>           registry, ingress and sinks included.</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span>Bus      — LocalBus matches published events to subscriptions,</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span>           returns (node, triggers) pairs (peregrin-bus).</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span>Runner   — owns the loop, capability checks, retry/circuit, timers,</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span>           ingress (peregrin-engine).</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span>Store    — the event log + run state + DLQ ports (peregrin-store).</span></span>\n<span class="line"><span>Observer — lifecycle hooks (peregrin-bus).</span></span></code></pre></div><p><strong>Branching lives in the driver</strong> (it decides what events to emit). <strong>Topology lives in the graph</strong> (on/emits contracts). Clean separation: the graph says <em>when</em>, the driver says <em>what happened</em>.</p>',23)])])}const g=n(o,[["render",i]]);export{u as __pageData,g as default};
